      <details class="about-section" id="overview" name="about" open>
        <summary>
          <h2>Overview</h2>
        </summary>
        <p>
          <strong>“Mapping Multilateral Geneva”</strong> is a digital humanities
          project, conceptualised by a research team at the{" "}
          <a href="https://www.graduateinstitute.ch/cdhm">
            Centre for Digital Humanities and Multilateralism (CDHM)
          </a>
          , Geneva, Switzerland. It brings together spatial and temporal data
          from diverse archival sources pertaining to the emergence of
          multilateral institutions in Geneva over the last century.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project makes a case for studying multilateralism, along with its
          attendant actors and institutions, at a critical historical juncture
          when institutions of international cooperation have increasingly come
          under scrutiny.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project is intended as a research tool for historians, research
          scholars interested in ideas of international cooperation, and for
          professionals in multilateral institutions. The focus on Geneva may
          also be of interest to individuals in the Swiss Romande region who
          wish to study the intersections between Genevan social history and
          multilateral organisations.
        </p>
        <p>
          Work on this project is ongoing and the database is being continuously
          updated. The latest version was updated on dd/mm/yyyy. We welcome
          suggestions, resources, and contacts that will help add new layers of
          details to this project. We are also keen to collaborate with
          researchers who may be interested in using data from this project for
          related realisations.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="mailto:cdhm@graduateinstitute.ch">
            cdhm@graduateinstitute.ch
          </a>
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="about-section" id="methodology" name="about">
        <summary>
          <h2>Methodology</h2>
        </summary>
        <h3>Categories</h3>
        <p>
          This project includes a selection of three categories of
          organisational entities that comprise “Multilateral Geneva”. These
          are:
        </p>
        <h4>Permanent Representations</h4>
        <p>
          Permanent representations are diplomatic missions in charge of
          following and participating in the work and facilitating the contacts
          between the respective national governments, representations, and
          between organizations. This map includes permanent representations at
          the League of Nations and at the United Nations.
        </p>
        <p>
          Although the term “permanent representation” was not used at the time
          of the League of Nations, the entries included under this category are
          representations of member states or observers that were officially
          accredited to the League as well as other permanent offices in direct
          contact with the Secretariat. These representations could have been
          headed by an ambassador or secretary. In some cases, the listed
          addresses were personal addresses of the representatives or were
          located in consulates.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the period after the establishment of the United Nations, based on
          the available Blue Books, the project includes:
        </p>
        <ol type="a">
          <li>
            <p>Permanent missions of Member states to the UN</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Non member states that have permanent observer status to the UN in
              Geneva
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Intergovernmental organisations that have established observer
              offices to the UN in Geneva
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Other organisations that have established observer offices to the
              UN in Geneva
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <h4>International Organisations</h4>
        <p>
          There is no universally accepted definition of what constitutes an
          international organisation. There are some “indicators” to be found in
          related legislation. These include organizations that are a) founded
          on an international agreement; b) consist of States and other
          entities; c) possess their own international legal personality; d)
          disposing of “at least one organ capable of expressing a will distinct
          from member states”.
        </p>
        <p>
          In this project, we have included intergovernmental organizations, UN
          system organizations, bodies and entities related to the UN system,
          secretariats, international treaties, and historically significant
          organisations and International organizations recognized by
          Switzerland (quasi governmental) under Article 60 of the Swiss Civil
          Code.
        </p>
        <p>
          We have also included organisations and bodies (e.g. international
          secretariats) with Special Status that do not match the all criteria
          to be defined as an international organisation, but play an important
          role in the International Geneva ecosystem and are granted special
          legal status by Swiss authorities.
        </p>
        <h4>Non-governmental Organisation</h4>
        <p>
          The category of non-governmental organisations (NGOs) includes
          private, non-profit organisations that operate independently of
          governments, typically with a humanitarian, advocacy, or social
          mission of international relevance, including religious relief
          organizations and international federations of civil society.
        </p>
        <p>
          We sought to identify organisations that were
          “internationally-oriented” and whose activities were of international
          relevance, ie, whose interest, activities, and management reflect
          participation across multiple countries. To a large extent, we have
          excluded organisations that represent specific professional interests,
          or focus on niche technical or medical issues.
        </p>
        <h4>
          <mark>Special Cases</mark>
        </h4>
        <p>
          <strong>The International Committee of the Red Cross</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC), established in
          1863, is a neutral, independent humanitarian organisation that
          predates the establishment of multilateral organisations in Geneva by
          over half a century. While the ICRC simultaneously fits and does not
          fit the criteria used in our methodology to be categorised as an
          International Organisation or a Non-governmental Organisation, its
          significance to the shaping of Geneva’s humanitarian traditions
          remains indisputable, and hence “special” in our categorisation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The Geneva Graduate Institute</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The Geneva Graduate Institute, where this project is based, is
          included as a special case on the map. The Institute’s establishment
          and history is linked directly to the establishment of the League of
          Nations and the need for experts trained in international affairs.
          Given the relevance of the institute and its predecessors, the
          Graduate Institute of International Studies (IUHEI) (1927-2007), and
          the Graduate Institute of Development Studies (IUED), (1977-2007) to
          the growth and development of internationalism over the years and the
          exchange of expertise between these institutions, we have located
          these three entities on our map.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="about-section" id="sources" name="about">
        <summary>
          <h2>Sources</h2>
        </summary>
        <p>
          The sources for this project can broadly be divided into two
          categories: those collections used systematically across multiple
          editions to form the base dataset, and those used primarily to fill
          specific gaps. The latter category of sources were consulted for
          recent decades where many organisations possessed online archival
          collections of various documents. Here, we only provide a full list of
          the former.
        </p>
        <h4>Archives</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>League of Nations Archives, United Nations Geneva, Geneva</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              UNOG Registry Fonds (1946-1973), United Nations Geneva, Geneva
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              United Nations Archives, Archives and Records Management Section
              (ARMS), New York
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              International Institute of Intellectual Co-operation, UNESCO
              Archives, Paris
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Published material</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <strong>
                The Union of International Associations’{" "}
                <em>Yearbook of International Organizations</em>
              </strong>
              . Available in the Geneva Graduate Institute Library, we relied
              most heavily on this corpus to collate the initial dataset,
              particularly of NGOs and IOs. Beginning in the 1940s and ending in
              2009, we extracted all of the information pertaining to
              organisation names, addresses, and representation in Geneva,
              proceeding periodically in intervals of 3-5 sources per decade.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <strong>
                The Centre for Research on International Institutions’{" "}
                <em>International Geneva Yearbook</em>
              </strong>
              . Available in the <em>Bibliothèque de Genève</em> and published
              between 1988-2009, this collection was conceived as a practical
              handbook for navigating International Geneva, and therefore
              provides a detailed account of many of its important institutions.
              Here too, the organisation names, addresses, and representative
              information was systematically extracted.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <strong>
                The <em>Annuaire Genevois</em>.
              </strong>
              <br />
              Available at the <em>Archives Cantonales de Genève</em> and
              published between 1828-1999, these were compiled by private
              publishing houses and provide granular, street-by-street
              information about the city. These were primarily utilised to
              complete missing information in our initial database. Editions
              between 1952-1970 also included lists of Geneva’s principal
              non-governmental organisations.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <strong>
                The United Nations{" "}
                <em>
                  Blue Books
                  <br />
                </em>
              </strong>
              Published from the early days of the UN, a full digital collection
              exists online from 1953 and official lists are available on-site
              at the UN archives for preceding years. We initially accessed
              these at 2-3-year intervals to extract all available address and
              representative information. We then returned to the interim years
              to complete any missing information and thus build a highly
              accurate picture of the permanent representations in Geneva across
              the period.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <strong>
                The League of Nations{" "}
                <em>
                  Handbook of International Organizations
                  <br />
                </em>
              </strong>
              Periodically published by the League of Nations from 1921 to 1938
              and digitally available on the UN Geneva Archives platform, these
              provide information about “unions, associations, institutions,
              commissions, bureaux” that are international “in their
              constitution and in their sources of financial support.” The
              handbooks were not published yearly and were based on information
              shared by the organisations themselves. The gaps were mitigated
              through information triangulation with archives.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>
                <strong>
                  Annuaire international genevois
                  <br />
                </strong>
              </em>
              First published in 1926<em>,</em> this contains a list of
              international organisations, non-governmental organisations and
              permanent representatives based in Geneva. It was published by the
              Permanent Centre of International Information (Centre permanent
              d’informations internationales) until the end of the 1930s.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <em>
                <strong>
                  Who’s Who
                  <br />
                </strong>
              </em>
              A digital repository maintained by the International Geneva
              Portal, this provides the current Geneva addresses of NGOs and
              IOs. We utilised this to complete our base dataset for the most
              recent period between 2010-2026.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <details class="about-section" id="accuracy" name="about">
        <summary>
          <h2>A Note on Accuracy and Consistency</h2>
        </summary>
        <p>
          Despite our best efforts, there are a number of ways in which data
          inaccuracies may persist on the map.
        </p>
        <h4>Source Gaps</h4>
        <p>
          Firstly, the data accuracy is dependent on information listed in the
          sources used. We have noted conflicting addresses and organisation
          information for the same year across multiple sources. This can be
          partially explained by the fact that in published material, there may
          be a time gap between a change of address or name, the effective
          notification, and publication of the source. Importantly, this means
          that the first appearance of an organisation in a source and therefore
          our map, does not necessarily correspond to the actual year when it
          was established in Geneva. The same applies for instances of
          dissolution, transfer or movement for other reasons and disappearance
          from the map. When this information is known, it has been specified in
          the information box. More frequently, especially in the case of larger
          organisations, conflicting address information can be explained by
          organisations possessing multiple addresses, typically a separate
          postal and office address. Where possible, the map has privileged the
          effective address of these entities.
        </p>
        <h4>Maps</h4>
        <p>
          <mark>
            We use{" "}
            <a href="https://map.geo.admin.ch/#/map?lang=en&amp;center=2500449.94,1117307.66&amp;z=7.538&amp;topic=swisstopo&amp;layers=ch.swisstopo.landeskarte-grau-10;ch.swisstopo.swissimage-product@year=2010;ch.swisstopo.hiks-siegfried@year=1939&amp;bgLayer=ch.swisstopo.pixelkarte-farbe&amp;featureInfo=default&amp;catalogNodes=swisstopo,1392">
              this dataset
            </a>{" "}
            from <em>swisstopo</em> as the base map to represent organisations
            present in this Geneva canton. The representation of data time
            stamps—called «journey through time» —in this dataset allows users
            to interactively explore the evolution of the landscape from 1926 to
            the present day along with the superimposed organisation dots.
          </mark>
        </p>
        <p>
          We use{" "}
          <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/#map=17/46.188027/6.154173">
            Open Street Maps
          </a>{" "}
          in the backend to verify and mark addresses as dots on the map. In the
          case of changed streets, buildings and landscape modification, we have
          marked the most proximate location within &lt;50 metres. In addresses
          that showed more than one result, exact map pins have also been
          triangulated with Google Maps (including street view).
        </p>
      </details>
      <details class="about-section" id="team" name="about">
        <summary>
          <h2>About the Team</h2>
        </summary>
        <p>
          <strong>Jacob Thornton</strong> is a PhD candidate in the Department
          of International History and Politics and a research affiliate with
          the Centre for Digital Humanities and Multilateralism. His recently
          concluded master’s thesis focused on Geneva’s ‘Association Maçonnique
          Internationale’, examining its contested position within the emerging
          institutional structures of interwar internationalism.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Dr. Pierre-Etienne Bourneuf</strong> is Head of Strategic
          Partnerships at Centre for Digital Humanities and Multilateralism. He
          had previously worked as Scientific Advisor at the United Nations
          Library and Archives Geneva where he contributed to the Centenary of
          Multilateralism Project and the LONTAD Project.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Sohnee Harshey</strong> is a PhD candidate in the Department
          of Anthropology and Sociology and a research affiliate with the Centre
          for Digital Humanities and Multilateralism. Earlier in her career in
          India, she was part of the founding team of Economic and Political
          Weekly’s digital initiative, Engage and led communications and
          advocacy for a feminist think tank, The Third Eye portal.
        </p>
      </details>
    </main>

    <script>
      // A link to a section (about/#sources, or the navigation above) opens
      // that section: a closed <details> does not open on its own anchor.
      const openTarget = () => {
        const target = document.getElementById(location.hash.slice(1));
        if (target instanceof HTMLDetailsElement) {
          target.open = true;
          target.scrollIntoView();
        }
      };
      addEventListener("hashchange", openTarget);
      openTarget();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
